    "excluded": "Semantic proof reconstruction/evaluation, Lean kernel checking, fresh condition proof generation, actual game execution, native execution, and common library code/proof sizes. These times cannot be compared directly to prior protocols with different phase boundaries.",
    "interpretation": "The general normalizer and chain-specialized derivation have different input scope. Measurements compare these algorithms on the MAC chain; they do not isolate dimension as a causal factor or establish a statistical performance result."
  },
  "scope": [
    "Tree-preserving translations concern this common local-rule presentation. No impossibility of advantages for other higher-dimensional proof systems is claimed.",
    "No theorem of global minimum proof size, semantic minimum condition support, optimal repair cost or generic compiler completeness is supplied.",
    "Compilation acceptance for the independently generated MAC tree and the general geometric tree is checked for stages 0..4. All-stages success of the producer is not proved.",
    "The same existing attack, query order, retained state/trace and 1/256 bound are used. This step introduces no new cryptographic bound or native execution/time guarantee.",
    "The specialized geometric frontend is the same ordinary derivation interpreted into the geometric model; exact agreement is intentional. Its timings are a control, not independent evidence of two proof-search algorithms.",
    "Whole-certificate decoding, semantic/kernel proof-checking costs, a finite mixed higher basis including probabilistic and computational transformations, and full copy/discard integration remain open."
  ],
  "novelty": {
    "status": "unconfirmed",
    "finding": "In this local-rule library the presented tree translations preserve data exactly. The measured chain specialization benefits both ordinary and geometric proof construction. Neither sharing nor this specialization establishes an exclusive dimensional advantage.",
    "next_evidence": "Formulate and prove a relative mixed coherence/repair theorem with explicit finite-overlap and termination hypotheses, then construct the required finite diagrams for a substantive cryptographic signature and compare against ordinary shared-lemma repair."
  },
